{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"Implemented option B+C (operator decision 2026-11-02). Memory, tracemalloc peak of binding_jira_key_map, each measured in a fresh process:\n- Synthetic store of the live shape (22.8 MB, 5,664 bindings with nested baseline entries, comment_ids, reverse): before 50.6 MB, after 45.7 MB on the first call; a repeat call in the same process 0.3 MB and under 1 ms (cache hit, no parse). Map identical before/after (same sha256 digest, 5,664 entries). Script: .rebar/scratch/a85e3cd3-esok/measure.py in the worktree.\n- Read-only copy check against the live bindings.json (23.8 MB, 5,690 bindings): old whole-file load 68.0 MB, new loader 47.5 MB, map identical.\nPlan-review advisories: (1) composer_edit._enforce_promote_only also calls binding_jira_key_map; it only tests membership in the fresh dict that function builds per call, so the cached reverse map is never exposed or mutated there, and freshness is the same stat-token check as every other caller. (2) Cache concurrency: a populate race in a threaded server is benign; both writers store an equal map for the same token and single dict assignment is atomic under the GIL, matching the _TOML_CACHE precedent. (3) The mechanism-delta ratchet exits 0 with no marker; a module-level cache dict is not one of its 7 censused kinds."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793643567198003001,"uuid":"52707de0-63c3-4ee2-8991-12f53af5b7dd"}